Developer Installation

  Requirements

    Ruby 1.9.3
    https://rvm.io/
    > rvm install 1.9.3
    > rvm use 1.9.3

    Dependencies (bundler)
    > gem install bundler
    > bundle install

Infrastructure

  bin/sinatra_server
    a webrick server (same as ruby lib/server.rb)

  bin/shotgun_server
    a development server that reloads all the 
    code every request (slow but useful)

  config.ru
    makes the application rackable 
    (e.g. you can drop it into passenger)

  lib/server.rb
    Start here.


Application

  Designed to handle the submission of fasta files
  to a sequence analysis application called spewey-ross.


  flow:
  / - input form
  /run - handles post from the input form
         runs spewey with given options
  /sequence(.*).fasta - the format of the results files

  Thoughts:
  since there is multi step processing, and spewey is slow
  we probably need to produce tickets and have background
  processing and progress checking.

  ticket = sha1(fasta_file + options)

  public/runs/#{ticket}/
    input.fasta
    primary_output.fasta
    secondary_output.fasta
    individual/
        a.fasta
        a.png
        b.fasta
        b.png
    all.zip

TODO:

  - Implement use of a dropdown list of genomes, initially use the AA 
    files in /nfs/seq/Data, specifically: drosoph.aa, ensembl_human.aa,
    ensembl_mouse.aa, yeast.aa.  I recommend we do an initial import 
    and "freeze" the .aa files along with their dates, for reproducibility.
    
    I envision the web front-end loading the information using a CSV
    text file with metadata that I will create in the following form:

    "filename","species","date","upstream_url"
    "/some/location/yeast.aa","S. cerevisiae","2013-09-12","ftp://genome-ftp.stanford.edu/pub/yeast/data_download/sequence/genomic_sequence/orf_protein"
    ...

    It would then load this metadata and use the columns to create a
    dropdown list in the web UI (or set of radio buttons, depending on
    the number of genomes we choose), where the button or list item
    would be: "S. cerevisiae protein sequences (2013-09-12)".  Ideally
    it would also include the upstream URL somewhere, but that's not
    needed initially.  Once the user has chosen the option, it would
    feed the local filename (on the server) to the "java -jar
    plaac.jar" call.

    Selecting a genome would be mandatory if a user enters proteins in
    the text box, no default would be provided and the "Run Analysis"
    option greyed out, forcing the user to select a species.

    The slider should only appear when the upload file option is chosen
    (if the slider is not shown, it will default to alpha=1).

    If upload file is chosen, then by default the slider should be set
    to alpha=0 (i.e. it uses the input file as the sole source of AA
    background information) and in this case, the species/genome
    options would be greyed out completely, because then we are using
    only the uploaded file as background information.

    If the slider is dragged somewhere between 0 < alpha < 1, then
    both a species/genome selection is mandatory and the file upload
    option must have been selected.  (This handled automatically
    because the slider is only ever shown if the file upload option is
    selected.)

